    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Did you have trouble paying attention like this as a child, before age 12?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, yes, actually. I mean, even back in grade school, it was always the same story. My mother saved all my report cards in this big cedar chest she kept at the foot of her bed. It smelled so strongly of mothballs, you know? She bought it at an estate sale from the Johnsons down the street when they moved to Ohio. Mr. Johnson had gotten a job at a tire factory, I think. Or maybe it was auto parts. \n\nBut anyway, those report cards... the teachers always wrote the exact same things. They'd say I was bright, but careless. Always losing my spelling papers or talking out of turn. Mrs. Gable in third grade—she had this enormous beehive hairdo that never moved an inch, even in a stiff breeze—she was always having to remind me to stay in my seat or finish my arithmetic. I just couldn't seem to help it. Do you remember those old wooden desks with the inkwells? They were so fascinating to look at, much more interesting than long division.",
